edit(p,'Build a new version and swap a pointer instead of editing in place.','Build a new version with a correct publication and lifetime protocol rather than editing published data in place.','Immutablealoneinsufficientpublishing.',c)
edit(p,'The printer is no longer contended.','Clients no longer directly acquire the physical printer; the queue/daemon still needs its own synchronization and back-pressure protocol.','Spoolingdoesnotremoveallcontention/deadlock.',s)
edit(p,'## Breaking no preemption\n\nIf a thread can\'t get the next lock, make it give up the ones it has. That is voluntary preemption, and it\'s the idea behind **try-lock with back-off**:','## Try-lock and rollback\n\nIf a thread cannot acquire the next lock immediately, it releases the ones it acquired for this attempt before retrying. This avoids blocking hold-and-wait; it does not forcibly preempt another owner\'s lock.','Voluntaryreleaseisnotbreakingnopreemption.',s)
edit(p,'def lock_both(a, b):\n    while True:','def lock_both(a, b):\n    if a is b:\n        raise ValueError("distinct locks required")\n    while True:', 'Samelockotherwiseinfiniteretry.',py)
edit(p,'Why it can\'t deadlock: a thread never *waits* while holding `a`.','Assume callers hold no other locks and successful callers eventually release both. This acquisition loop avoids a blocking cycle because it never waits for b while holding a.', 'Otherheldlocks/badcalleroutsideprotocol.',s)
edit(p,'The random sleep (jitter) breaks the symmetry;','Random sleep can break the symmetry but gives no deterministic fairness bound;', 'Jitternotguaranteedprogress.',s)
edit(p,'def transfer(src, dst, amount):\n    first, second','def transfer(src, dst, amount):\n    if src is dst:\n        return\n    first, second', 'Demonstratedcorrecttransferhandlesalias.',py)
edit(p,'Sort by a stable key such as an account id. Sorting by memory address (`id()` in Python, pointer value in C) works within one process, and the Linux kernel often does this when it must lock two objects of the same type. It doesn\'t work across processes or machines, where addresses differ.','Use a stable total ordering that distinguishes distinct lock objects, such as unique immutable account IDs. Python id() orders live objects within a process. In portable C, relational comparison of pointers to unrelated objects is not a valid general ordering rule; use explicit keys or a documented platform convention. Address order is not a distributed identity.', 'CpointerrelationalUBandtiesequalIDs.',c+py)
edit(p,'| Try-lock + back-off | No preemption | Livelock risk |','| Try-lock + back-off | Blocking hold-and-wait | Livelock risk |','CorrectCoffmanclassification.',s)
edit(p,'Try-lock with back-off breaks no preemption, but needs randomised back-off to avoid livelock.','Try-lock with release-on-failure avoids blocking hold-and-wait. Jitter can reduce livelock risk but does not guarantee fairness.', 'Correctsummaryclassificationandprogress.',s)
qe(q,'dl-prevention-q3','explanation','A failed try-acquire returns False. Python Lock.release raises RuntimeError if the lock is unlocked, not merely because another thread acquired it: primitive Lock has no owner.','Pythonownerfalse.',py,option=2)
qe(q,'dl-prevention-q3','workedExample','The try-acquire returns False without waiting, then a is released before retry. Under the assumption of no other held locks, this removes blocking hold-and-wait for the pair. It does not forcibly take a resource from another holder. Random delay reduces synchronized retries but guarantees neither bounded waiting nor success.','FalseCoffmanandjitterguarantee.',s)
qe(q,'dl-prevention-q6','explanation','Self-deadlock can involve one thread. Here the LevelLock checker instead rejects the decreasing acquisition level before attempting the second lock.','Originalagreeddeadlockrequires2threads.',s,option=0)
qe(q,'dl-prevention-q10','text','The required shard is not known initially; locking every possible shard would be conservative but expensive, so discovering and revalidating the set or imposing an order is preferable','Allshardsnotonlysafealternative.',s,option=3)
qe(q,'dl-prevention-q10','explanation','The direct one-pass up-front scheme lacks its required lock set. It could over-lock, or use a separate discovery/revalidation protocol; this makes it a less natural fit than a known global order.','Do notclaimmustlockall.',s,option=3)
qe(q,'dl-prevention-q10','workedExample','The shard depends on protected state. A naive request-everything-before-reading scheme would take a large conservative set. Alternatives include reading the shard, releasing, acquiring the discovered set consistently and revalidating, or defining users before shard locks. Revalidation must itself respect that order; do not acquire users after shard if that reverses it.','Originalsuggestedreverseorderrecheckdeadlock.',s)
qe(q,'dl-prevention-q5','text','Use a correctly published and safely retained immutable snapshot so readers need no lock on that resource','Snapshotpublication/lifetimenecessary.',c,option=1)
